  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-105502",
    "knowledge_point_id": "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/7b44dcfdfef91ada119ea7346b59f337a82bc8cca1e364fd6ea29c9eead5a2fd.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-3)=-2$ and $g(-3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,-2)$ equals $3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-3)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & ={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
